A1. Three dozen of these are plenty for a young woman. ~ BIRTHDAYS
A9. We are looking forward to this. ~ FUTURE
A10. This describes some speakers and also some soups. ~ LOUD
A12. This will help some. ~ REMEDY
A13. A place where 3 is a crowd. (plur.) ~ COUPES
A15. This used to trail around in the gay nineties. ~ HEM
A16. This is part of us. ~ ME
A17. This was found loaded in Chicago. ~ VAN
A18. If your clock does this when you look at it, you had better change the face. ~ STOP
A20. Here are some little gems. ~ OPALS
A22. This is simple. ~ MERE
A23. A metal container. ~ ORE
A24. One of the four horsemen made a hit in this. ~ POLO
A25. This one likes fine feathers and is always confronted with a bill. ~ BIRD
A26. It was on account of these they stuck together. ~ GLUES
A28. Before. ~ ERE
A29. This is often a dark secret. ~ PAST
A30. Keep this under your hat. ~ HAIR
A31. Try this for a little change. (abbr.) ~ CT
A32. This one takes care of the little things. ~ NURSEMAID
A34. A combination of types. ~ PI
A35. The sound part of the automobile industry. ~ HORN
A36. Brought into the world. ~ BORN
A37. A wheel projection. ~ CAM
A38. What success will do for one. ~ ELATE
A40. This is the big thing for the farmer this year. ~ CROP
A41. This is just half. ~ DEMI
A42. This has ripping teeth. ~ SAW
A43. Two tons. ~ LOAD
A44. Farmers use these for the soil. ~ SOAPS
A45. A good catcher. ~ TRAP
A47. Poetical anger. ~ IRE
A48. The way aviators' wives look. ~ UP
A49. Rested. ~ SAT
A50. Starved. ~ DIETED
A52. These haven't a leg to stand on. ~ SNAKES
A54. This will smooth things out. ~ IRON
A55. This kind of a day brings out the crowds at the ball games. ~ LADIES
A56. This is what influenced the jury. ~ TESTIMONY

D1. This fellow used to get quite a kick out of summer hotels. ~ BUM
D2. These often get in the society columns. ~ ITEMS
D3. The kind of people that are always getting to the front. ~ RUDE
D4. Attempt. ~ TRY
D5. This stands for the boy friend. ~ HE
D6. The way some people play solitaire. ~ ALONE
D7. Another part of us. ~ YOU
D8. This is what the family does at tea-time. ~ SUPS
D9. This one is always going for the mail or male. (variant sp.) ~ FEMALE
D11. This is what makes motorists go wrong. ~ DETOUR
D12. This is what they heard about Chicago. ~ REPORT
D13. This is what the nervy salesman handed the boss. ~ CARD
D14. The way some jockeys felt after the Kentucky derby. ~ SORE
D15. A pre-nuptial affair. ~ HOPECHEST
D17. These make the back-bone of the American people. ~ VERTEBRAE
D19. This one never wears "a rainbow round his shoulders." ~ PESSIMIST
D21. Behold! ~ LO
D22. What they used to call Mrs. Hoover before she was married. ~ MISS
D25. An equine garage. ~ BARN
D26. This is what the scales show up. ~ GAIN
D27. They are trying to put this on in Chicago. ~ LID
D29. This is genuine. ~ PURE
D30. What we wish some bridge players were playing. ~ HARP
D32. A negative. ~ NOT
D33. We hope this will be happy in the morning. ~ MOOD
D34. Treelees plains in South America. ~ PAMPAS
D35. Hula-hula land. ~ HAWAII
D37. Traffic never does this on some streets. ~ CEASES
D39. This is always fat. ~ LARD
D40. This makes a wood-pile. ~ CORD
D41. To perform. ~ DO
D43. They are putting these on all the new buildings. ~ LIENS
D44. Where castles are built. ~ SPAIN
D46. This is very saucy. ~ PERT
D48. To put in reverse. ~ UNDO
D51. The location of the first hole. ~ TOE
D52. Your uncle. ~ SAM
D53. If this is hard to get in the hole something is wrong with your club. ~ KEY
D55. This is a short way of making a Long Island. ~ LI


